Enzyme rule data attribution
============================

GlycoEnzDB-derived rules
------------------------

The ordinary enzyme rules distributed with glyenzy, other than the
sulfotransferase rules described below, were selected and translated from
GlycoEnzDB:

https://virtualglycome.org/glycoenzdb/

GlycoEnzDB is described in:

Zhou et al., "GlycoEnzDB: A database of enzymes involved in human
glycosylation", Glycobiology.
https://doi.org/10.1093/glycob/cwaf074

GlycoEnzDB states that its material is licensed under the Creative Commons
Attribution 4.0 International license (CC BY 4.0), except for textbook figures
from Essentials of Glycobiology:

https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

glyenzy adapts the selected reactions into its IUPAC-condensed enzyme-rule
format and does not redistribute those textbook figures.

GlycoEnzOnto-derived sulfotransferase rules
-------------------------------------------

The human N- and O-glycan sulfotransferase rules distributed with glyenzy were
selected and translated from GlycoEnzOnto:

https://github.com/neel-lab/GlycoEnzOnto

GlycoEnzOnto is described in:

Huang et al. (2022), "GlycoEnzOnto: a glycan-domain ontology for modeling
knowledge of glycan biosynthesis", Bioinformatics.
https://doi.org/10.1093/bioinformatics/btac704

The source material is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
International license (CC BY 4.0):

https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

For glyenzy, the source reactions were selected to retain canonical human
N- and O-glycan contexts, translated into glyenzy's IUPAC-condensed enzyme-rule
format, expanded from compact alternatives into explicit rules, and supplied
with explicit context requirements where needed. Reactions limited to
glycosaminoglycans or O-Xyl glycans, glycolipids, protein sulfation, sulfate
removal, donor synthesis, or transport were excluded.

Abstract N-glycan reaction rules
--------------------------------

The separate abstract_enzymes() collection translates the simplified Golgi
reaction table supplied by the user on 2026-08-31. The model and its branch
notation are described in:

Spahn et al. (2016), "A Markov chain model for N-linked protein
glycosylation - towards a low-parameter tool for model-driven
glycoengineering", Metabolic Engineering.
https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ymben.2015.10.007

Liang et al. (2020), "A Markov model of glycosylation elucidates isozyme
specificity and glycosyltransferase interactions for glycoengineering",
Current Research in Biotechnology.
https://doi.org/10.1016/j.crbiot.2020.01.001

These are independently encoded reaction facts, not a reproduction of the
table image or article text. The supplied table takes precedence over
differences in published versions, including the beta1-2 GnTI product.
Original abbreviated reaction strings are retained alongside the translated
IUPAC rules in data-raw/glyenzy_abstract_enzymes.json. Fixed, site-specific
reject motifs preserve the ordered ManII steps and prevent the first iGnT
extension on the alpha1-3 arm in networks generated by this collection from
the default precursor. They do not encode arbitrary ancestor paths or node
degrees: external inputs with pre-extended alpha1-3 antennae or extra branches
can escape these terminal rejects.

GlcH is an added abstract activity combining the three existing MOGS/GANAB
precursor glucose-removal rules attributed to GlycoEnzDB above. It retains
one-residue reaction steps rather than introducing a lumped transformation.
